P has the same x-coordinate as D, which is 8.\n\nLine EF: E = (152/13, 72/13), F = (24/5, 32/5).\nDirection: F - E = (24/5 - 152/13, 32/5 - 72/13) = ((312 - 760)/65, (416 - 360)/65) = (-448/65, 56/65).\nParametrize: P = E + t(F - E).\nx-coordinate: 152/13 + t·(-448/65) = 8.\n152/13 = 760/65. So 760/65 - 448t/65 = 8 = 520/65.\n448t = 240 → t = 240/448 = 15/28.\n\ny-coordinate of P: 72/13 + (15/28)·(56/65) = 72/13 + (15·56)/(28·65) = 72/13 + (15·2)/(65) = 72/13 + 30/65 = 72/13 + 6/13 = 78/13 = 6.\n\nSo P = (8, 6).\n\nLine AP: A = (9,12), P = (8,6). Direction: P - A = (-1, -6).\nParametrize: (9 - t, 12 - 6t). At y=0: 12 - 6t = 0 → t = 2. x = 9 - 2 = 7.\nSo Q = (7, 0).\n\nNow ω₁ passes through B=(0,0) and is tangent to AQ at Q=(7,0). Also ω₁ meets AB again at X.\n\nThe line AQ has direction (-1,-6) from A, or equivalently the line through A(9,12) and Q(7,0). The slope of AQ: (0-12)/(7-9) = -12/-2 = 6. So line AQ: y = 6(x - 7), i.e., y = 6x - 42.\n\nω₁ is tangent to line AQ at point Q. So the center of ω₁ lies on the line perpendicular to AQ through Q. The perpendicular to AQ (slope 6) has slope -1/6. Through Q(7,0): y = -(1/6)(x-7).\n\nCenter O₁ = (h, -(h-7)/6) for some h. Also O₁ is equidistant from B and Q (since circle passes through both B and Q... wait, does it pass through Q? It's tangent to AQ at Q, so yes Q is on the circle).\n\nActually, the circle is tangent to AQ at Q, meaning Q is the point of tangency, so Q is on the circle. And B is on the circle. So O₁ is equidistant from B and Q.\n\n|O₁B|² = h² + (-(h-7)/6)² = h² + (h-7)²/36\n|O₁Q|² = (h-7)² + (-(h-7)/6)² = (h-7)² + (h-7)²/36 = (h-7)²·(37/36)\n\nSetting equal:\nh² + (h-7)²/36 = (h-7)²·37/36\nh² = (h-7)²·37/36 - (h-7)²/36 = (h-7)²·36/36 = (h-7)²\nh² = (h-7)²\nh = ±(h-7)\nCase 1: h = h-7 → 0 = -7, impossible.\nCase 2: h = -(h-7) = 7-h → 2h = 7 → h = 7/2.\n\nSo h = 7/2. O₁ = (7/2, -(7/2-7)/6) = (7/2, -(-7/2)/6) = (7/2, 7/12).\n\nRadius² = |O₁Q|² = (7/2 - 7)²·37/36 = (-7/2)²·37/36 = (49/4)·(37/36) = 49·37/144.\nRadius = 7√37/12.\n\nNow ω₁ meets AB again at X. Line AB: from B(0,0) to A(9,12). Parametrize as (9t, 12t) for t ∈ [0,1]. B is at t=0.\n\nCircle ω₁: (x - 7/2)² + (y - 7/12)² = 49·37/144.\nSubstitute x = 9t, y = 12t:\n(9t - 7/2)² + (12t - 7/12)² = 49·37/144\n(9t - 7/2)² = 81t² - 63t + 49/4\n(12t - 7/12)² = 144t² - 14t + 49/144\nSum = 225t² - 77t + 49/4 + 49/144 = 225t² - 77t + 49(36+1)/144 = 225t² - 77t + 49·37/144.\nSet equal to 49·37/144:\n225t² - 77t = 0\nt(225t - 77) = 0\nt = 0 (point B) or t = 77/225.\n\nSo X = (9·77/225, 12·77/225) = (693/225, 924/225) = (231/75, 308/75) = (77/25, 308/75).\nLet me simplify: 693/225 = 231/75 = 77/25. 924/225 = 308/75.\nX = (77/25, 308/75).\n\nNow ω₂ passes through C=(14,0), tangent to AQ at Q=(7,0). Center O₂ on perpendicular to AQ through Q: y = -(x-7)/6.\nO₂ = (h, -(h-7)/6).\nEquidistant from C and Q:\n|O₂C|² = (h-14)² + (h-7)²/36\n|O₂Q|² = (h-7)²·37/36\n(h-14)² + (h-7)²/36 = (h-7)²·37/36\n(h-14)² = (h-7)²·36/36 = (h-7)²\nh-14 = ±(h-7)\nCase 1: h-14 = h-7 → -14 = -7, impossible.\nCase 2: h-14 = -(h-7) = 7-h → 2h = 21 → h = 21/2.\n\nO₂ = (21/2, -(21/2-7)/6) = (21/2, -(7/2)/6) = (21/2, -7/12).\nRadius² = (21/2-7)²·37/36 = (7/2)²·37/36 = 49·37/144. Same radius.\n\nω₂ meets AC again at Y. Line AC: from C(14,0) to A(9,12). Parametrize: (14 - 5t, 12t) for t ∈ [0,1]. C at t=0.\nCircle ω₂: (x - 21/2)² + (y + 7/12)² = 49·37/144.\nx = 14 - 5t, y = 12t.\n(14 - 5t - 21/2)² + (12t + 7/12)² = 49·37/144\n(7/2 - 5t)² + (12t + 7/12)²\n= 49/4 - 35t + 25t² + 144t² + 14t + 49/144\n= 169t² - 21t + 49/4 + 49/144\n= 169t² - 21t + 49·37/144\nSet = 49·37/144:\n169t² - 21t = 0\nt(169t - 21) = 0\nt = 0 (point C) or t = 21/169.\n\nY = (14 - 5·21/169, 12·21/169) = (14 - 105/169, 252/169) = ((2366 - 105)/169, 252/169) = (2261/169, 252/169).\nSimplify 2261/169: 169·13 = 2197, 2261 - 2197 = 64. So 2261/169 = 13 + 64/169. Hmm, let me check if it simplifies. 2261 = 7·323 = 7·17·19. 169 = 13². No common factors. So Y = (2261/169, 252/169).\n252 = 4·63 = 4·9